# Accessibility Standards

Follow these standards to ensure your application is accessible to all users.

## WCAG Guidelines

- Follow WCAG 2.1 Level AA guidelines as a minimum.
- Ensure sufficient color contrast (4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text).
- Provide text alternatives for non-text content.
- Ensure all functionality is keyboard accessible.

## Semantic HTML

- Use semantic HTML elements (nav, main, article, section, etc.).
- Use proper heading hierarchy (h1, h2, h3, etc.).
- Use appropriate form labels and inputs.
- Use ARIA attributes when semantic HTML isn't sufficient.

## Keyboard Navigation

- Ensure keyboard navigation works throughout the application.
- Provide visible focus indicators.
- Ensure logical tab order.
- Implement keyboard shortcuts for common actions.
- Don't trap keyboard focus unnecessarily.

## Screen Readers

- Test with screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver).
- Provide proper ARIA labels and descriptions.
- Use ARIA live regions for dynamic content updates.
- Ensure form errors are announced to screen readers.
- Provide skip links for navigation.

## Visual Accessibility

- Don't rely solely on color to convey information.
- Ensure interactive elements are clearly identifiable.
- Provide sufficient touch target sizes (minimum 44x44px).
- Support zoom up to 200% without horizontal scrolling.
- Ensure text is readable at different sizes.
